-- MRCP Part 2: Medical ophthalmology (batch 1)
-- 50 questions covering acute visual loss, diabetic eye disease,
-- uveitis & inflammatory eye disease, vascular & neuro-ophthalmology.

  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 72-year-old woman presents with sudden painless loss of vision in her right eye over the preceding 2 hours, associated with a 3-week history of temporal headache, jaw claudication and a tender scalp. She has lost 4 kg in weight over 2 months. Visual acuity is perception of light only in the right eye, with a relative afferent pupillary defect. Fundoscopy shows a pale, swollen optic disc. ESR is 98 mm/hr and CRP is 145 mg/L. She was started on IV methylprednisolone 500 mg daily 6 hours ago after the diagnosis was suspected clinically.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['giant cell arteritis','arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy','temporal artery biopsy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hypertension and atrial fibrillation (on apixaban) presents with sudden, painless, complete loss of vision in his left eye 90 minutes ago. Examination shows a cherry-red spot at the macula with surrounding retinal whitening and a relative afferent pupillary defect. Visual acuity is hand movements only. He arrived at the emergency department within 100 minutes of symptom onset.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['central retinal artery occlusion','ocular emergency','acute management']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 67-year-old man with hypertension and hyperlipidaemia reports sudden painless loss of the inferior half of his visual field in the right eye 2 days ago, described as a curtain coming down from above. Fundoscopy shows a flame-shaped haemorrhage pattern with dilated, tortuous retinal veins in the superior retina, and cotton wool spots. Visual acuity is 6/24. There is no relative afferent pupillary defect. Optical coherence tomography shows macular oedema.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial management strategy?',
  array['branch retinal vein occlusion','macular oedema','anti-VEGF therapy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 45-year-old man with poorly controlled type 1 diabetes and known severe non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy presents with sudden painless loss of vision in his left eye over several hours. He describes floaters and a shower of black spots preceding the loss of vision. Fundoscopy is difficult due to media opacity, but a red reflex is present and B-scan ultrasonography shows dense vitreous echoes without retinal detachment.',
  'What is the most likely diagnosis and most appropriate next step?',
  array['vitreous haemorrhage','proliferative diabetic retinopathy','B-scan ultrasound']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 29-year-old woman with a body mass index of 38 presents with a 3-week history of headache worse on lying flat and coughing, transient visual obscurations lasting seconds, and pulsatile tinnitus. Visual acuity is preserved at 6/6 bilaterally but visual field testing shows enlarged blind spots. Fundoscopy reveals bilateral optic disc swelling. MRI and MR venography of the brain are normal. Lumbar puncture with opening pressure of 38 cmH2O confirms the diagnosis, and cerebrospinal fluid composition is normal. She has been started on acetazolamide 500 mg twice daily and weight loss advice, but 6 weeks later her visual fields have deteriorated further with new inferior nasal field loss despite good compliance and maximally tolerated acetazolamide dosing.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['idiopathic intracranial hypertension','progressive visual field loss','optic nerve sheath fenestration']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 34-year-old woman presents with a 4-day history of pain on eye movement and blurred vision in her left eye, now reduced to 6/36 with reduced colour vision and a relative afferent pupillary defect. She reports a similar episode of numbness in her right leg 18 months ago that resolved spontaneously. MRI of the brain and orbits shows a demyelinating plaque within the left optic nerve and several periventricular white matter lesions consistent with disseminated disease.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management of her current visual symptoms?',
  array['optic neuritis','multiple sclerosis','corticosteroid therapy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and obstructive sleep apnoea (non-compliant with CPAP) wakes with painless loss of vision in the inferior half of his visual field in the right eye. Visual acuity is 6/12. Fundoscopy shows a swollen, hyperaemic optic disc with a few peripapillary flame haemorrhages, without a pale disc appearance. There are no systemic features of giant cell arteritis, and inflammatory markers are normal. He is 58 years old and has no history of temporal headache or jaw claudication.',
  'What is the most appropriate management approach?',
  array['non-arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy','vascular risk factor modification','sleep apnoea']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 26-year-old woman with known systemic lupus erythematosus (on hydroxychloroquine 400 mg daily for 8 years, and mycophenolate for lupus nephritis) presents with a 2-day history of painless blurred central vision in both eyes. Fundoscopy shows a subtle bull's-eye pigmentary maculopathy. Humphrey visual field testing shows bilateral paracentral scotomas, and spectral-domain OCT shows loss of the parafoveal ellipsoid zone ('flying saucer' sign). Her estimated cumulative hydroxychloroquine exposure is calculated at 6.8 mg/kg/day based on her actual body weight.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['hydroxychloroquine retinopathy',$x$bull's-eye maculopathy$x$,'drug toxicity']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 24-year-old woman presents with a 5-day history of severe unilateral eye pain, redness, photophobia, and blurred vision. She wears monthly disposable contact lenses and admits to occasionally sleeping in them. Slit-lamp examination reveals a central corneal ulcer with a ring-shaped stromal infiltrate and significant epithelial defect, with radial perineural infiltrates noted on further examination. Corneal scrapings are sent for culture, and initial treatment with intensive topical fluoroquinolone has failed to improve symptoms after 72 hours.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management given the lack of response to initial antibiotic therapy?',
  array['microbial keratitis','acanthamoeba keratitis','contact lens wear']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 70-year-old man with known bilateral neovascular age-related macular degeneration, previously stable on intravitreal aflibercept every 8 weeks in his right eye, presents with a 3-day history of worsening distortion and central vision loss in that eye. Amsler grid testing confirms new metamorphopsia, and OCT shows new subretinal fluid with an increase in central subfield thickness compared with his previous scan taken 6 weeks ago, consistent with disease reactivation ahead of his scheduled injection.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['neovascular age-related macular degeneration','anti-VEGF treatment failure','treat-and-extend regimen']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 19-year-old man sustains a blow to the right eye during a football match. He presents with pain, diplopia on upward gaze, and reports nausea. Examination shows restricted upward gaze in the right eye, enophthalmos, and hypoaesthesia over the right cheek and upper lip. CT of the orbits shows a fracture of the orbital floor with herniation of orbital fat and the inferior rectus muscle into the maxillary sinus, with a 'trapdoor' fracture pattern in this young patient.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['orbital blowout fracture','trapdoor fracture','inferior rectus entrapment']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 63-year-old woman with hypertension presents with sudden painless visual loss affecting the entire visual field of her right eye. Visual acuity is counting fingers. Fundoscopy shows widespread retinal haemorrhages in all four quadrants ('blood and thunder' appearance), dilated tortuous retinal veins, and disc swelling. There is a relative afferent pupillary defect. She is reviewed 10 weeks later with new painful red eye, raised intraocular pressure of 42 mmHg, and new vessels visible at the iris margin and in the angle on gonioscopy.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management of the raised intraocular pressure and iris neovascularisation?',
  array['central retinal vein occlusion','neovascular glaucoma','panretinal photocoagulation']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'mrcp2', '32511ae4-eff2-4f6c-b6ac-ca8673db9378',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 38-year-old woman with a 10-year history of type 1 diabetes and stage 4 chronic kidney disease undergoes rapid correction of severe hyperglycaemia (presenting glucose 32 mmol/L) as an inpatient with insulin infusion, and 5 days later develops new blurred vision bilaterally. Fundoscopy shows worsening of pre-existing mild non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy with new cotton wool spots but no evidence of proliferative changes or macular oedema on OCT.',
  'What is the most likely explanation for this deterioration, and what is the most appropriate management?',
  array['diabetic retinopathy','rapid glycaemic correction','early worsening phenomenon']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 54-year-old man with type 2 diabetes of 15 years' duration and an HbA1c of 78 mmol/mol has centre-involving diabetic macular oedema in his left eye, with visual acuity of 6/18 and central subfield thickness of 420 micrometres on OCT. He has no history of cardiovascular disease. He is keen to avoid frequent hospital visits given work commitments.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?',
  array['diabetic macular oedema','anti-VEGF therapy','centre-involving oedema']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 61-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and centre-involving diabetic macular oedema has been treated with monthly intravitreal aflibercept for 6 months (6 injections) in his right eye. Visual acuity has improved only marginally from 6/24 to 6/21, and central subfield thickness on OCT remains largely unchanged at 380 micrometres. He is phakic with no history of glaucoma.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['diabetic macular oedema','anti-VEGF non-response','switching therapy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 47-year-old man with type 2 diabetes attends routine diabetic eye screening. Retinal photographs show extensive intraretinal microvascular abnormalities (IRMA), venous beading in two quadrants, and multiple blot haemorrhages in all four quadrants, but no definite new vessels. Visual acuity is unaffected and there is no macular oedema on OCT.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['severe non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy','screening intervals','risk of progression']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 58-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and high-risk proliferative diabetic retinopathy (new vessels at the disc greater than one-third disc area, with vitreous haemorrhage) in her left eye has just completed a full panretinal photocoagulation regimen 2 weeks ago. She now reports worsening central vision and new metamorphopsia. OCT shows new centre-involving macular oedema that was not present prior to laser treatment, with central subfield thickness increased from 260 to 340 micrometres.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['panretinal photocoagulation','post-laser macular oedema','combination therapy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 66-year-old man with proliferative diabetic retinopathy and a non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage present for 3 months despite conservative management undergoes pars plana vitrectomy with endolaser panretinal photocoagulation. Six weeks postoperatively, he develops rubeosis iridis and intraocular pressure of 38 mmHg, with gonioscopy confirming new vessels in the angle, despite the endolaser having been applied extensively at the time of surgery.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['post-vitrectomy neovascular glaucoma','incomplete panretinal photocoagulation','anti-VEGF adjunct']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 32-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes since childhood, currently 14 weeks pregnant with her first child, has moderate non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy on her booking eye examination, which was mild at her last screening 18 months ago before pregnancy. Her glycaemic control has improved markedly since conception (HbA1c falling from 86 to 58 mmol/mol).',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her diabetic eye surveillance during this pregnancy?',
  array['diabetic retinopathy in pregnancy','rapid glycaemic optimisation','screening frequency']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 49-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and proliferative diabetic retinopathy with tractional retinal detachment threatening but not yet involving the fovea is reviewed in clinic. Visual acuity is 6/9 and OCT confirms epiretinal membrane and vitreoretinal traction with a shallow detachment extending to within 500 micrometres of the fovea. He has no vitreous haemorrhage.',
  'What is the most appropriate management approach?',
  array['tractional retinal detachment','vitrectomy timing','diabetic retinopathy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 44-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and stable proliferative diabetic retinopathy previously well-controlled with panretinal photocoagulation reports gradually worsening night vision and peripheral visual field constriction over the past 2 years, confirmed on Goldmann visual field testing showing significant peripheral field loss with preserved central acuity of 6/9.',
  'What is the most likely explanation for these symptoms?',
  array['panretinal photocoagulation complications','peripheral field loss','night vision impairment']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 71-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes, ischaemic heart disease (myocardial infarction 8 months ago), and centre-involving diabetic macular oedema is being considered for intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy. Her cardiologist notes she remains at elevated cardiovascular risk. She asks whether the injections carry any risk related to her heart condition.',
  'What is the most appropriate advice regarding anti-VEGF therapy in this context?',
  array['anti-VEGF systemic safety','cardiovascular risk','arterial thromboembolic events']
),
