  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and proliferative diabetic retinopathy is treated with intravitreal ranibizumab monotherapy (rather than panretinal photocoagulation) for new vessels at the disc, following a shared decision-making discussion given her occupation as a long-haul lorry driver and concerns about peripheral field loss from laser. She attends for her scheduled injection but discloses she has missed her last two follow-up visits due to work commitments and is unsure whether she will attend reliably going forward.',
  'What is the most important consideration in her ongoing management?',
  array['anti-VEGF monotherapy for PDR','treatment adherence risk','panretinal photocoagulation as fallback']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000026', 'mrcp2', '8d8740ea-0a53-48f6-9a4a-f8c480fccd15',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and mild non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy in both eyes is found on routine screening to have a small area of non-centre-involving diabetic macular oedema in his right eye, with visual acuity of 6/6 unaffected and no symptoms.',
  'What is the most appropriate management approach at this stage?',
  array['non-centre-involving macular oedema','observation versus treatment threshold','screening follow-up']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000027', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 34-year-old man with HLA-B27-associated recurrent acute anterior uveitis has had 5 episodes in his right eye over the past 18 months, each treated successfully with intensive topical corticosteroids and cycloplegics but recurring within weeks of stopping treatment. He has no systemic inflammatory disease diagnosed despite rheumatology review, and is otherwise well. He asks about reducing the frequency of these episodes.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management to reduce recurrence?',
  array['recurrent anterior uveitis','HLA-B27','long-term topical prophylaxis']
),
