  'draft', 'core',
  'A 29-year-old woman with juvenile idiopathic arthritis-associated chronic anterior uveitis, previously well-controlled on methotrexate, presents with a quiet white eye but is found on routine slit-lamp screening to have new fine keratic precipitates and 2+ anterior chamber cells, asymptomatic. She has band keratopathy and early posterior subcapsular cataract noted on examination, both attributed to chronic disease and prior corticosteroid use.',
  'What is the most important management consideration highlighted by this case?',
  array['JIA-associated uveitis','asymptomatic disease activity','routine screening importance']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000031', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 68-year-old man presents with acute unilateral panuveitis, hypopyon, and rapidly progressive vision loss 4 days after uncomplicated cataract surgery. He reports increasing pain and redness since day 2 post-operatively. Visual acuity has fallen to counting fingers, with marked vitritis obscuring fundal view. B-scan ultrasound shows dense vitreous echoes without retinal detachment.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['acute postoperative endophthalmitis','intravitreal antibiotics','ophthalmic emergency']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 45-year-old woman with birdshot chorioretinopathy (HLA-A29 positive), on mycophenolate mofetil for 18 months with previously stable disease, is found to have new multifocal cream-coloured choroidal lesions and worsening electroretinogram parameters, alongside new symptoms of nyctalopia and worsening visual field constriction, despite good compliance with treatment.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['birdshot chorioretinopathy','electroretinogram monitoring','immunosuppression escalation']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 31-year-old man presents with acute unilateral anterior uveitis, his second episode in the same eye in 2 years, associated with lower back pain and stiffness worse in the mornings that improves with exercise, present for over 3 months. He has no known systemic diagnosis. Examination confirms circumcorneal injection, fine keratic precipitates, and 2+ anterior chamber cells with no posterior segment involvement.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step alongside treating the acute uveitis?',
  array['acute anterior uveitis','inflammatory back pain','ankylosing spondylitis screening']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 26-year-old woman with acute anterior uveitis is started on intensive topical dexamethasone drops. At review 3 weeks later, her anterior chamber activity has resolved, but intraocular pressure has risen from a baseline of 15 mmHg to 32 mmHg in the affected eye, with early glaucomatous optic disc changes not previously present.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['steroid-induced ocular hypertension','uveitis treatment complication','steroid tapering']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 39-year-old man with HIV infection, poorly compliant with antiretroviral therapy (CD4 count 42 cells/microlitre), presents with painless progressive blurred vision in his right eye over 2 weeks. Fundoscopy shows a full-thickness area of retinal necrosis with granular yellow-white infiltrate and associated haemorrhage, described as having a 'pizza pie' appearance, following the vascular arcades, with minimal vitreous inflammation.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['CMV retinitis','HIV-associated opportunistic infection','antiviral therapy']
),
('d8000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000036', 'mrcp2', '0acc1b57-6242-4999-a4ea-16a4a59d4d65',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 55-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis of 20 years' duration, previously on long-term methotrexate and now escalated to a biologic agent 6 months ago for refractory joint disease, presents with a painful red eye with severe scleral tenderness, deep episcleral vessel injection that does not blanch with topical phenylephrine, and a small area of scleral thinning with visible underlying uveal tissue. Vision is unaffected.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate approach to management?',
  array['necrotising scleritis','rheumatoid arthritis','systemic immunosuppression escalation']
),
